course: dictionary
lesson: 9457
title: Two meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Stardust can be a science word, or a dreamy word from stories. The words around it tell you which meaning you need.
explain: In science, «we are made of stardust» means the atoms in our bodies were made inside stars long ago. When those stars died, they spread dust across space, and that dust is stardust.
explain: In stories and songs, stardust means magic, sparkle or charm, like a sky full of dreams. Ask yourself: is the sentence about space and science, or about wonder and magic? That question tells you which meaning to use.
fact: Stardust is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say some stardust, not two stardusts.
fact: Science meaning: dust made in old stars and spread in space when they die.
fact: «Many elements in our bodies, like carbon and oxygen, were made inside ancient stars.»
fact: Literary meaning: a magical, dreamy or romantic quality, like sparkle or charm.
fact: To check a meaning, swap it into the sentence. If the sentence still makes sense, you have chosen well.
exercise: In science class, Mr Patel says «we are made of stardust». What does stardust mean here? | A sparkly powder used in fairy tales | Sand from the seaside | Glitter used for school art projects | *Dust made in old stars and spread in space when they die
exercise: Lila says «the old song was full of stardust». Which meaning fits best? | A type of rock | Dirt on a window | Dust made in old stars | *Magic, sparkle and dreams
exercise: Copy one word from «Many elements in our bodies, like carbon and oxygen, were made inside ancient stars» that means very old. | oxygen | bodies | *ancient | carbon
test: Jayden says «we are made of stardust» is just a silly saying. Is it true? | *It is true: the atoms in our bodies were made inside stars | It means our skin is made of sand | It is false: stars never make anything | It means we are covered in glitter
test: In a bedtime story, «the night sky shimmered with stardust». Which meaning fits? | Space dust from dead stars, measured in a lab | A type of cloud that brings rain | Dirty air from a factory | *Magic, sparkle and dreamy charm
exam: Which is the best way to work out which meaning of stardust a sentence needs? | *Read the words around it and swap each meaning in | Skip the word and never come back | Look only at the title of the book | Guess the first meaning you see every time
